  {
    id: "soc-st-2-s7t1",
    title: "Major Revolutions in History",
    subject: "Social Studies",
    subjectIcon: "🔥",
    programme: "Both",
    unitId: "social-studies",
    difficulty: 3,
    estimatedMinutes: 18,
    xpReward: 45,
    prerequisites: ["soc-st-2-s6t2"],
    shsLevels: ["SHS 2"],
    suggestedLevel: "SHS 2",
    steps: [
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-intro", type: "info", content: "**Major Revolutions in History**\\\\n\\\\nRevolutions are fundamental and rapid changes in political, social, or economic systems. Throughout history, revolutions have transformed societies and shaped the world we live in today.\\\\n\\\\nIn this lesson, we will explore some of the most significant revolutions in world history and their impact." },
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-content-1", type: "info", content: "**The Agricultural Revolution (c. 10,000 BCE)**\\\\n\\\\nThe transition from hunting and gathering to settled agriculture.\\\\n\\\\n**Key Changes:**\\\\n• Humans began domesticating plants and animals\\\\n• Permanent settlements replaced nomadic lifestyles\\\\n• Food surplus led to population growth\\\\n• Specialisation of labour (crafts, trade, governance)\\\\n• Development of writing and record-keeping\\\\n\\\\n**Impact:** Laid the foundation for civilisation itself, including the rise of cities, trade, and organised government.\\\\n\\\\n**The Industrial Revolution (1760–1840)**\\\\n\\\\nThe shift from manual labour to machine-based manufacturing.\\\\n\\\\n**Key Changes:**\\\\n• Invention of steam engine, spinning jenny, power loom\\\\n• Rise of factories and mass production\\\\n• Urbanisation as people moved to cities for work\\\\n• Improved transportation (railways, steamships)\\\\n• New social classes (industrial workers and capitalists)\\\\n\\\\n**Impact:** Transformed economies, societies, and daily life. Led to modern capitalism, but also to poor working conditions and environmental pollution." },
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-content-2", type: "info", content: "**The French Revolution (1789–1799)**\\\\n\\\\nA revolution that overthrew the French monarchy and established a republic.\\\\n\\\\n**Causes:**\\\\n• Extreme inequality between the rich nobility and poor commoners\\\\n• Financial crisis and heavy taxation\\\\n• Enlightenment ideas of liberty, equality, fraternity\\\\n• Food shortages and economic hardship\\\\n\\\\n**Key Events:**\\\\n• Storming of the Bastille (July 14, 1789)\\\\n• Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen\\\\n• Reign of Terror under Robespierre\\\\n• Rise of Napoleon Bonaparte\\\\n\\\\n**Impact:** Spread ideas of democracy, nationalism, and human rights across Europe and the world.\\\\n\\\\n**The Russian Revolution (1917)**\\\\n\\\\nOverthrew the Russian monarchy and established the world's first communist state.\\\\n\\\\n**Causes:**\\\\n• Autocratic rule of the Tsar\\\\n• Defeat in World War I\\\\n• Economic hardship and food shortages\\\\n• Rise of revolutionary ideas (Marxism)\\\\n\\\\n**Impact:** Led to the creation of the Soviet Union, inspired communist movements worldwide, and shaped 20th-century geopolitics." },
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-content-3", type: "info", content: "**Other Important Revolutions**\\\\n\\\\n**The American Revolution (1775–1783):**\\\\n• Thirteen American colonies fought for independence from Britain\\\\n• Led to the creation of the United States of America\\\\n• Established democratic principles and a constitution based on separation of powers\\\\n\\\\n**The Haitian Revolution (1791–1804):**\\\\n• Enslaved Africans in Haiti revolted against French colonial rule\\\\n• Successfully abolished slavery and established an independent nation\\\\n• The only successful slave revolt in history\\\\n\\\\n**The Scientific Revolution (16th–17th Century):**\\\\n• Transformation in scientific thinking — Copernicus, Galileo, Newton\\\\n• Shift from religious to empirical explanations of the natural world\\\\n• Laid the foundation for modern science and technology\\\\n\\\\n**The Digital Revolution (Late 20th Century):**\\\\n• Rise of computers, the internet, and digital technology\\\\n• Transformed communication, work, education, and entertainment\\\\n• Continues to reshape every aspect of modern life" },
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-exercise", type: "question", content: "Test your understanding:", exercise: { question: "Which revolution is associated with the slogan 'Liberty, Equality, Fraternity'?", options: ["American Revolution", "French Revolution", "Russian Revolution", "Industrial Revolution"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The French Revolution was inspired by Enlightenment ideas of liberty, equality, and fraternity." } },
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-summary", type: "info", content: "✅ **Key Takeaways: Major Revolutions in History**\\\\n\\\\nIn this lesson, you learned about:\\\\n• The Agricultural, Industrial, and Digital Revolutions\\\\n• The French, American, Russian, and Haitian Revolutions\\\\n• The causes, key events, and impacts of each revolution\\\\n\\\\nRevolutions have profoundly shaped the political, social, and economic systems of the modern world." },
      { id: "soc-st-2-s7t1-checkpoint", type: "checkpoint", content: "Check your understanding", checkpoint: { title: "Major Revolutions — Quick Check", passThreshold: 60, bonusXp: 20, questions: [ { question: "What was a major cause of the French Revolution?", options: ["Too much equality", "Extreme inequality and financial crisis", "Industrialisation", "Foreign invasion"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The French Revolution was caused by extreme inequality between classes, financial crisis, and Enlightenment ideas." }, { question: "What is the significance of the Haitian Revolution?", options: ["It was the first revolution in Europe", "It was the only successful slave revolt in history", "It started the Industrial Revolution", "It ended colonialism in Africa"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The Haitian Revolution was the only successful slave revolt in history, leading to the establishment of an independent Haiti." } ] } }
    ]
  },

